#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# all_true

#% text
# `all_true(xs, f)` is `true` when `f(x)` is `true` for every element `x` of `xs`.

#% text
# `all_true(xs, f, n)` tests the parts down to level `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# An empty list, or an atom, gives `true`.

#% item
# The values of an association are its elements.

#% item
# An answer that is neither `true` nor `false` leaves a conjunction of the open tests.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
all_true([2, 4, 6], is_even)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
all_true([2, 3], is_even)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Down to level 2:

#% cell
all_true([[2, 4], [6, 8]], is_even, 2)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The values of an association:

#% cell
all_true(assoc(a -> 1, b -> 3), is_odd)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Operator form:

#% cell
all_true(is_prime)([2, 3, 5])
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `any_true`, `none_true`, `select`
